# Session affinity (replaces Julay Channel values)

- `session` transition/constructor modifier marks exclusive pairwise protocols.
- `session` is incompatible with `provider`/`client`/`internal`; all offers of an action must agree on the session tag.
- If two proc classes share any session action, they must not also share an ordinary (untagged) action. Ordinary sync with a different peer class, and `provider`/`client` APIs (even when a session peer is also a client), remain allowed.
- Affinity and SyncChannel sessions are **per-Proc local** only — never a Program-global registry. Session engage installs the peer `Proc` handle in local affinity (for `killSessionPeer`); no live-proc lookup on Program.
- First contact: peers rendezvous on the static (global) action channel; Program creates exactly one dedicated SyncChannel in `SyncPayload.sessionToInstall`. Subsequent offers use that dedicated channel. Dedicated syncs install nothing.
- Session constructor: sync size 1 on the global channel; spawn installs follow-on dedicated sessions before the child starts.
- Session constructor exclusivity: while affinity to a peer of that child class is live, a second session-ctor spawn throws `JulayException` (no implicit guards; action stays user-enabled). After peer exit closes sessions and scrub clears affinity, a later spawn may establish again.
- Session key: `(peerProcId, sessionActionName)` → dedicated `SyncChannel` (sync size 2).
- Peer exit closes shared dedicated channels; surviving Select exits on close, scrubs affinity, and may re-handshake on the global channel. Proc exits only when no actions are enabled.
- Mid-life session teardown via effect builtins (transitions only): `exitSession(PeerClass)` / `killSessionPeer(PeerClass)` take a **bare leaf proc-class name**. Lookup affinity by `Proc.className`; **no-op** if that peer affinity is absent (never throw). On match: `exitSession` closes dedicated channels and clears affinity (both procs keep running); `killSessionPeer` does the same then silently cancels the peer proc’s job. Codegen emits `hostProc.exitSession("PeerClass")` / `hostProc.killSessionPeer("PeerClass")`.
- Untagged default = ordinary non-sticky rendezvous; `service` = shared multiplexed API.

## TLA+ encoding

- Two-sided session specs emit `session_*`, `CanStartSession_*`, `EndSession_*`, and `*_dead`.
- `sessionException` (init FALSE) and `SessionIntegrity == ~sessionException` (checked in the `.cfg`) are emitted **only** when a session-constructor (hybrid) action exists — models rebind `JulayException`. Transition-only session specs omit them.
- SpecLeaves always have `*_constructed`. `*_killed` (init FALSE) is emitted for leaves that are a `killSessionPeer` peer target **or** that call `exitProc` somewhere in the module; those leaves gate actions with `~killed`, and their `*_dead` includes the kill disjunct. Specs with neither omit all `*_killed`.
- Transition-only session actions: sticky `(session \/ CanStart)` under `\* Session connection semantics`.
- Session ctor-bearing (hybrid) actions: under the same comment, `IF CanStart THEN` session assign + spawn updates `ELSE sessionException' = TRUE` (no spawn) — mirrors runtime `JulayException` on rebind.
- `exitProc()`: action sets caller `*_killed' = TRUE` (self-exit); runtime is `hostProc.requestSilentKill()`. Banned in procfun bodies at compile time.
- `exitSession(Peer)` / `killSessionPeer(Peer)`: resolve session pair as caller ↔ named peer leaf; `IF anyLive THEN lookup /\ clear (and kill) ELSE UNCHANGED` — no-op when affinity absent; when some peer session exists, the quantified index must match. When the same pair is torn down on a sticky session action, skip `session' = TRUE` and leave clearing to the effect block. Natural `EndSession_*` remains for enablement death.
